Done. Round 19 (V1–V5) is implemented and both commands are clean.

**What it does** — `bun src/cli.ts evaluation ingest <verdict.json> --run DIR [--session S]`: each FAIL requirement becomes one failure (`found_by: evaluator`, `check: evaluator:<id>`, input the id, expected PASS, actual FAIL) whose evidence file holds the id and the repro of every FAIL case of its group; each UNKNOWN becomes one line `-<id> · UNKNOWN · <reason>` in `DIR/evaluator-notes.md`; a PASS writes nothing; the same verdict's bytes are ingested once; a missing/unparseable/`requirements`-less verdict is a `- evaluation · could not run · …` note, exit 1, no failure. I confirmed the failure reaches the stop: `insight --session s1` lists it open, so the insight is required before stopping.

**Verification** — `bun test` → exit 0, last line `Ran 1524 tests across 37 files. [22.78s]` (0 fail; the 7 round19 tests included). `tsgo --noEmit -p .` → exit 0, no output. Nothing staged or committed; no test or SPEC touched.

**Changes** — [src/evaluation.ts](/home/smoke01/dev/ancient-games/.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/evaluation.ts) (new, 216 lines) and +25 lines in [src/cli.ts](/home/smoke01/dev/ancient-games/.claude/skills/workflow-design/guard/src/cli.ts:619) (command, usage line, dispatch case; no other line changed).

**Contradictions EJ should look at** — the group→case join is written down nowhere: `tools/evaluator/verdict.py:256` joins by `startswith(group + "-F")`, but the test's fixture writes its FAIL case as `G1-c1`, so the writer's own rule matches nothing there and V1's evidence assertion could not hold; I matched the prefix `<group>-`, which covers both. And where the once-per-verdict hash is kept is my choice, not the SPEC's: `types.ts` is fixed so no new journal record kind was available, so the hashes go in a new `.harness-guard/evaluations.jsonl` — with the consequence that the same bytes ingested under a second `--run` or `--session` records nothing. Two unnamed cases also went to judgement: a requirement with no id is treated as V4's could-not-run (otherwise `validateFailure` rejects it and the CLI would exit 2), and a FAIL with an empty reason takes the SPEC's default while an UNKNOWN with no reason writes the line without invented text. Beyond the tests I exercised each early return by hand in scratch `/tmp` roots — empty reason, a 210-char repro scrubbed to `[228 chars]` in the evidence, same id with different bytes, same bytes again, and `--run` outside the root (exit 2, nothing written).

